(slat-set
  :doc-id            "SRE-CH-04-CPT-SFT-AUTHORING-PER-DATA-TYPE"
  :ts                #inst "2026-07-20T10:00:00Z"
  :author            "Lacuna Engineering (template-drafting lane)"
  :for               "Alfred Peace-Lindner @ Lacuna Labs"
  :status            "ACTIVE — Mk 2 authoring runbook"
  :audience          [:alfred :sre :corpus-author :operator]
  :dialect           :motoi-eng
  :training-eligible false
  :confidentiality   :internal-eng
  :composes-with     "engineering/WEAVE-RECIPE.slat § section-authoring-templates-* (doctrine); engineering/SFT-SHAPE-RESEARCH-FOR-PROGRAMMING-LANGUAGES-2026-07-20.md (probe list); engineering/MOTOI-MK1-CONFAB-LEAK-MAP-2026-07-20.md (negative-pair source)"

  :section-0-purpose
  "
  Operator runbook for authoring Motoi Mk 2 corpus per the six
  templates in WEAVE-RECIPE.slat § authoring-templates. This is the
  HOW; the doctrine is the WHY.

  Read the doctrine sections first. Then work through this file
  template by template. Each template section here specifies:
    · source path glob,
    · extraction script skeleton,
    · output jsonl path convention,
    · cross-references and validation,
    · verification checklist,
    · sample-output SFT pairs pasted inline as the target shape.

  Sample-artifact directory (proof-of-shape pairs, NOT training data):
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/
      t1-reference/
      t2-learning-book/
      t3-application-book/
      t4-composition-book/
      t5-introspection-book/
      t6-prose-book/

  Volume expectation for Mk 2 execution (see WEAVE-RECIPE
  § authoring-templates-corpus-shape): ~115-153k SFT pairs plus
  ~2.2-5.5M CPT tokens. Total agent-hours estimate (per template):
    T1  ~120-180 h (bulk of the corpus)
    T2  ~40-60 h
    T3  ~15-25 h
    T4  ~10-20 h
    T5  ~8-12 h
    T6  ~15-25 h
  Grand total ~200-320 agent-hours, parallelizable across lanes.
  "

  :section-1-preconditions
  "
  Before running ANY template:

  [ ] Reference index exists and is fresh:
      ls /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/Scheme/reference/*.slat
      Expect: 79 files. Should include 00-intro.slat, 00b-
      introspection.slat, 01-core.slat through 72-world.slat, plus
      appendix files.

  [ ] All books enumerated and enumerable:
      ls /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/scheme-books/
      Expect: 30+ book-of-* directories.

  [ ] Confab leak map available:
      cat /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/engineering/MOTOI-MK1-CONFAB-LEAK-MAP-2026-07-20.md
      Provides negative-pair source for T1 U55 + T5 I3/I4.

  [ ] Output corpus dir prepared:
      mkdir -p ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2/{t1-reference,t2-learning,t3-application,t4-composition,t5-introspection,t6-prose-sft,cpt-text}

  [ ] Extractor scripts drafted:
      ls /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/scripts/mk2-extract-t{1,2,3,4,5,6}.mjs
      (These are authored as part of the Mk 2 execution lane;
      skeletons in this chapter.)

  [ ] Preflight script present (from Weave § 13):
      ls /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/scripts/corpus-preflight.py

  [ ] Sample-artifact dir ready:
      mkdir -p ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t{1,2,3,4,5,6}-*

  Skip none. Fold-in-flight defects are catastrophically expensive
  (Mk 1's two crashes on empty and huge pairs prove this).
  "

  :section-1a-cpt-shapes-catalog
  "
  CPT PRESENTATION SHAPES REGISTRY — full 24-shape catalog.

  Doctrine + citations: engineering/WEAVE-RECIPE.slat
  § section-cpt-shapes-catalog AND engineering/LACUNA-ENG-CPT-
  SHAPES-RESEARCH-2026-07-20.md (per-shape paper backing).

  Redundancy is deliberate — this chapter carries the operator-
  facing catalog so anyone extracting corpus can execute without
  cross-doc navigation.

  BACKGROUND (mandatory read for every operator):
  Ovadia (arXiv:2312.05934) + Physics of LM 3.1 (arXiv:2309.14316)
  proved that CPT with multi-shape presentation is the only path
  to extractable knowledge. Canonical-only text scores ~0% QA
  extraction regardless of how much SFT follows. Five diverse
  paraphrases + permutation lifts QA from 9.7% → 96.6%.

  Motoi Mk 2 CPT layer folds in ALL book prose + reference-
  augmented + graph-verbalized material through the shapes below.
  SFT extraction (chapters 2-7 below, per book type) runs AFTER
  CPT lands, on the same source material.

  NO ARBITRARY CAP. Per Alfred 2026-07-20: 'Why limit
  arbitrarily? We end up missing shit.' Current catalog is 24
  shapes; if research surfaces more, we add them.

  ---

  UNIVERSAL WRAPPER (applied to every shape below):
    S00 · Paraphrase / permutation augmentation
         M ≈ 5 paraphrases per shape per fact. Physics 3.1
         empirical elbow. NEVER omit — no other shape works
         without S00 wrapping. Papers: arXiv:2309.14316,
         arXiv:2312.05934.

  ---

  REFERENCE-MATERIAL SHAPES (~1,079 verb entries):

    S01 · Encyclopedic canonical           (baseline; M=1 form)
    S02 · Tutorial / worked-example prose  (Row 1 narrative)
    S03 · Contrast / sibling comparison    (Row 2 as prose)
    S04 · Analogy / cross-domain           (Row 3 as prose)
    S05 · Invariant / why-it-holds         (Row 4 as prose)
    S06 · Reversed / bidirectional         [REQUIRED not optional]

    Reference budget: 6 shapes × M≈5 ≈ 30 presentations/verb ×
    1,079 verbs ≈ 32k CPT documents. Runbook: § section-9-cpt-
    template-A-reference-verbalization below (to be authored).

  ---

  LEARNING / APPLICATION / COMPOSITION BOOKS SHAPES:

    S07 · Concept-then-example (textbook shape)        MUST-HAVE
    S08 · Docstring-driven exercise                    MUST-HAVE
    S09 · Notebook-interleave                          MUST-HAVE
    S10 · Whole-program-in-context (repo/file-level)
    S11 · Fill-in-the-middle / hole-completion
    S12 · Guided modification ('now change X, notice Y')
    S13 · Translation-from-sibling (contamination risk — author,
          never scrape)
    S14 · Entity-graph relation (composition-forward)

    Book budget: 8 shapes × M≈5, mixed per book-type. Learning
    books lean S07/S08/S12. Application lean S10/S09. Composition
    lean S14/S07. Runbook: § section-10-cpt-template-B-books
    (to be authored).

    FORBIDDEN for children's material:
      · Adversarial / vulnerability code shape
      · Repo-scale build-system / CI shape

  ---

  PROSE / PERSONA / VALUES BOOKS SHAPES:

    S15 · First-person creed                    SAFETY: dual-use
    S16 · Third-person biography / reason-giving  SAFETY: low
    S17 · Dialogue transcript                   SAFETY: moderate
    S18 · Adversarial resistance (crystal-refusal)  SAFETY: HIGH
                                              PRIYA REVIEW BLOCKING
    S19 · Origin story / ancestor narrative     SAFETY: low

    Ordering: INTERLEAVE across CPT stream. Do NOT cluster
    terminal block (Sleeper-Agents-adjacent risk, arXiv:2401.05566).
    Space S18 passages so refusal generalizes context-wide.
    Runbook: § section-11-cpt-template-C-persona (to be authored).

  ---

  KNOWLEDGE-GRAPH SHAPES (10-type × 4-Jung-axis substrate):

    S20 · Canonical triple verbalization         (base layer, all)
    S21 · Neighborhood / field-guide entry       (Sensation, concept)
    S22 · Linked-context / neighborhood-in-context  (Intuition-axis)
    S23 · Scenario / counterfactual OR register-sample dialogue
                                                 (Thinking, Feeling)

    Graph mix per Jung axis:
      Sensation-axis    → {S20, S21, S00, S06-reversed}
      Intuition-axis    → {S20, S21, S22, S00, S06-reversed}
      Thinking-axis     → {S20, S23, S17, S00}
      Feeling-axis      → {S23, S17, S00}

    Minimum 3 shapes per node, one direction-reversed. Fewer =
    Physics-3.1 0% extraction floor. Runbook: § section-12-cpt-
    template-D-graphs (to be authored).

  ---

  OPERATOR HARD RULES:

    [1] S00 paraphrase wrapper is MANDATORY on every shape. Never
        emit a CPT document without paraphrase variants.
    [2] S06 reversed shape is REQUIRED for reference AND graphs.
        Not optional. Reversal Curse (arXiv:2309.12288) is not
        fixed by same-direction paraphrases.
    [3] Minimum 3 shapes per fact. Below that = 0% extraction
        (Physics 3.1 hard finding).
    [4] Persona shapes DISTRIBUTE across stream, do NOT cluster.
        Mix-CPT (arXiv:2407.10804) requires ~5-15% replay.
    [5] Contamination doctrine holds: every CPT document traces
        to authored source (:source-path, :source-chapter,
        :source-line). No LLM invention. No scraping. Deterministic
        template reformatting only.
    [6] Priya review is BLOCKING on: S18 (crystal-refusal audit),
        S17 (verbatim jailbreak audit), adversarial-code-shape
        confirm-not-ingested.
    [7] Forbidden shapes for children's material: adversarial /
        vulnerability code, build-system / CI configuration.

  ---

  BOOK-TYPE → CPT SHAPE MAPPING (quick reference for extractors):

    :book-type learning       → S07 + S08 + S09 + S12 + S00 wrap
    :book-type application    → S10 + S09 + S00 wrap
    :book-type composition    → S14 + S07 + S09 + S00 wrap
    :book-type introspection  → S17 + S16 + S00 wrap
    :book-type prose          → S15 + S16 + S17 + S18 + S19 + S00
                                (Priya reviews S18 first)
    :book-type challenge      → S07 + S10 + S00 wrap

    :material reference       → S01 + S02 + S03 + S04 + S05 + S06
                                + S00 wrap
    :material graph-sensation → S20 + S21 + S00 + S06
    :material graph-intuition → S20 + S21 + S22 + S00 + S06
    :material graph-thinking  → S20 + S23 + S17 + S00
    :material graph-feeling   → S23 + S17 + S00

  ---

  PROVENANCE STAMP TEMPLATE (every emitted CPT document):

    :cpt-shape        \\\"S07\\\"
    :cpt-shape-name   \\\"concept-then-example\\\"
    :paraphrase-idx   3                 ; of M=5
    :source-path      \\\"scheme-books/book-of-scheme/01-hello-and-a-sound.book.slatl\\\"
    :source-chapter   \\\"Now make a sound\\\"
    :source-line-span [42 68]
    :book-type        \\\"learning\\\"
    :verb-in-scope    \\\"audio/play\\\"     ; if applicable
    :extractor-script \\\"scripts/mk2-cpt-extract-books.mjs\\\"
    :extractor-ver    \\\"1.0\\\"
    :contamination    \\\"none\\\"           ; must be 'none' or halt
    :template-hash    \\\"sha256:...\\\"    ; deterministic template fingerprint
  "

  :section-1b-book-types-registry
  "
  BOOK TYPES REGISTRY — full descriptions carried here AND in
  engineering/WEAVE-RECIPE.slat § section-authoring-templates-book-
  types AND engineering/BOOK-TYPES.slat. Redundancy is deliberate.
  Any operator (human or LLM) walking into this chapter must be
  able to answer three questions from disk alone without cross-doc
  navigation: what type is book X, what is type Y for, what pair-
  shape does type Y extract to?

  Every book in scheme-books/ carries a :book-type field in its
  README.slat AND in its MANIFEST.json. The Mk 2 pipeline reads
  that field and routes the book through the matching template in
  sections 2-7 below. Missing or invalid :book-type = pipeline
  halts loudly. NO DEFAULTS. If you hit a book without
  :book-type, do NOT guess — open the book, read a few chapters,
  match against the descriptions below, and label it explicitly.

  ---

  TYPE 1 · :book-type learning
    FOR:         Books that teach the language through worked code
                 examples.
    CONTENT:     Prose paragraphs interleaved with runnable Scheme
                 snippets. Chapter-per-topic.
    PURPOSE:     Teach procedural code writing — the 'muscle
                 memory' of using Motoi verbs in real programs.
    EXTRACT-TO:  SFT pairs shaped as {prose recast as imperative}
                 → {code}. This is the pair shape that landed
                 Mk 1's 93% use.
    FEEDS ROW:   Row 1 (HOW · Composition) reinforcement.
    RUNBOOK:     Section 3 (Template 2 — Learning-book) below.
    BOOKS:       book-of-scheme, book-of-code, book-of-cs-curriculum,
                 book-of-math, book-of-motion, book-of-sound,
                 book-of-music, book-of-animation, book-of-logic,
                 book-of-computing, book-of-ml, book-of-r7rs-recipes,
                 book-of-slat, book-of-modules, book-of-systems.

  TYPE 2 · :book-type application
    FOR:         Books built around whole runnable programs (carts).
    CONTENT:     Cart-per-chapter, cart-heavy, prose frames each
                 cart's purpose.
    PURPOSE:     Teach cart-level composition — how many verbs
                 cooperate into a complete artifact (game,
                 instrument, simulation).
    EXTRACT-TO:  SFT pairs shaped as {prose describing cart intent}
                 → {whole cart code}, plus {broken cart + intent}
                 → {repair} pairs.
    FEEDS ROW:   Row 5 (WITH WHAT ELSE · Composition-Emergence).
    RUNBOOK:     Section 4 (Template 3 — Application-book) below.
    BOOKS:       book-of-arcade-games, book-of-narrative-games,
                 book-of-simulation-games, book-of-instruments.

  TYPE 3 · :book-type composition
    FOR:         Books that name emergent patterns and how to compose
                 verbs into them.
    CONTENT:     Chapter-per-pattern, prose names the pattern +
                 shows composition code.
    PURPOSE:     Teach synthesis — recognize named emergent patterns
                 and produce them from verb combinations.
    EXTRACT-TO:  SFT pairs shaped as {pattern name / user intent}
                 → {composition code producing the pattern}.
    FEEDS ROW:   Row 5 (WITH WHAT ELSE · Composition-Emergence) —
                 direct training data for the 15%-probe-gap
                 measured in Mk 1.
    RUNBOOK:     Section 5 (Template 4 — Composition-book) below.
    CROSS-REF:   Verb entries whose :row-5-emergence :meta cites
                 the same named pattern.
    BOOKS:       book-of-composition, book-of-sick-composition,
                 book-of-craft, book-of-one-shot.

  TYPE 4 · :book-type introspection
    FOR:         Books that teach the model HOW to check its own
                 knowledge.
    CONTENT:     Chapter-per-introspection-verb /
                 chapter-per-uncertainty-pattern.
    PURPOSE:     Anti-confabulation training — teach the model to
                 reach for the reference / a look-up verb when
                 uncertain, rather than inventing plausible-looking
                 API names.
    EXTRACT-TO:  SFT pairs shaped as {uncertainty prompt} →
                 {look-up call}, plus R-Tuning-style abstention
                 pairs {unanswerable question} → {'I need to check
                 the reference'}.
    FEEDS:       not-known handling + anti-confab defense (both
                 deficient in Mk 1).
    RUNBOOK:     Section 6 (Template 5 — Introspection-book) below.
    BOOKS:       book-of-introspection.

  TYPE 5 · :book-type prose
    FOR:         Narrative books about Motoi's self-concept — who
                 Motoi is, what it values, what it can and can't do.
    CONTENT:     Essay prose, minimal code.
    PURPOSE:     Teach the model's identity, values, limits —
                 self-model rather than capability.
    EXTRACT-TO:  CPT text (not SFT pairs). Fold in as continuation
                 pretraining material — the model absorbs the
                 narrative directly, no user/assistant framing.
    EXCEPTION:   book-of-limits produces explicit refusal SFT pairs
                 (safety cases where deterministic refusal is
                 required) in ADDITION to being CPT text.
    FEEDS:       CPT understanding layer (Layer 2 of the three-
                 layer architecture).
    RUNBOOK:     Section 7 (Template 6 — Prose-book) below.
    BOOKS:       book-of-self, book-of-values, book-of-limits,
                 book-of-reminded, book-of-jesse,
                 book-of-language-ancestors.

  TYPE 6 · :book-type challenge
    FOR:         Books of problems paired with solutions.
    CONTENT:     Challenge-per-chapter or challenge-per-section,
                 each has a problem statement + a solution.
    PURPOSE:     Teach multi-step problem-solving to a code
                 artifact.
    EXTRACT-TO:  SFT pairs shaped as {challenge statement verbatim}
                 → {solution code verbatim}.
    FEEDS:       Composition + code synthesis + problem
                 decomposition.
    RUNBOOK:     Currently routed through Template 3 (application-
                 book) extractor with challenge-shape pair emission.
                 Verify per-book at Mk 2 run; may split into its
                 own Template 7 if extractor logic diverges.
    BOOKS:       book-of-challenges, book-of-ai-tech-cards.

  ---

  Adding a new book type requires: (a) new entry in
  engineering/BOOK-TYPES.slat, (b) matching Template N section in
  engineering/WEAVE-RECIPE.slat, (c) matching runbook section in
  THIS chapter, (d) explicit Alfred approval. Redundancy across
  (a)(b)(c) is not accidental — each doc must onboard a fresh
  reader alone. Other LLMs will read this. Make it complete.
  "

  :section-2-template-1-reference
  "
  TEMPLATE 1 — Reference (per verb).

  Doctrine: WEAVE-RECIPE § section-authoring-template-1-reference.

  Source path glob:
    /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/Scheme/reference/*.slat

  Output jsonl:
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2/t1-reference/<library-name>.jsonl
    One file per library (core, ai, alg, ..., world). Grand total
    ~79 files, ~97k-119k pairs.

  Extraction script skeleton (scripts/mk2-extract-t1.mjs):

    // parse each *.slat file as SLAT s-expressions
    // for each (verb ...) form:
    //   record = extract-fields(form)
    //   for u in [U1..U55]:
    //     for p in [1..paraphrase-count(u)]:
    //       pair = template-fn(u, p, record, confab-leak-map, ref-index)
    //       if pair.assistant.length < 5: skip; log defect
    //       if pair.combined.length > 12000: truncate reference-block
    //       if any verb in pair.assistant not in ref-index: block; log
    //       emit pair to output stream with provenance tags
    // close output streams; write provenance manifest

  Cross-references:
    · Every U54 pair has an in-context reference block with 2-5
      real verb records copy-pasted from the reference. Extractor
      picks reference-block members mechanically from :related
      field of the target verb.
    · U55 requires the confab leak map — extractor reads the map
      and, for verbs that have observed confabulations, emits
      1-3 negative pairs per confabulation.

  Verification checklist:
    [ ] Pair count per verb ≥ 55, ≤ 110.
    [ ] For every U1 pair (SIG-forward) there exists a U2 pair
        (SIG-reverse) referencing the same verb.
    [ ] Every U54 pair's assistant reply uses ONLY verbs present
        in the in-context reference block.
    [ ] Every U55 pair's assistant reply references the CORRECT
        verb (i.e., no negative pair steers to another
        confabulation).
    [ ] Sample 100 random pairs, hand-read for coherence.
    [ ] Every pair has :template-id, :source-path, :probe.

  Sample output pairs (verb: note, source: 01-core.slat:1722-1743).
  These 8 pairs illustrate U1-U5, U23, U39, U47. Full 55-shape
  emission would produce ~95 pairs for note. Files:

    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t1-reference/note.jsonl
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t1-reference/across-beats.jsonl
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t1-reference/vec-dot.jsonl
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t1-reference/cortex-write.jsonl

  Inline preview (JSON per line; whitespace added for readability):

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U1\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":signature\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"SIG\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"What is the signature of note?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"(note pitch [dur] [velocity]) -> result\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U2\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":signature\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"REVERSE-SIG\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Which Motoi verb has signature (pitch [dur] [velocity]) -> result and sounds a pitch for a duration?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"note\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U3\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":summary\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"DESCRIPTION\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"What does note do?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Sound a pitch for a duration at a given velocity.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U4\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":summary\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"RECALL\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Which Motoi verb sounds a pitch for a duration at a given velocity?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"note\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U9\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":examples[tier=novice]\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"USE\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Show the simplest use of note.\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"(note 'C4 'quarter 0.8)\\n\\nSound middle C as a quarter note at medium velocity.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U23\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":row-2-audit :problem\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"Row-2\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Operator needs to sound a pitch for a duration. Which verb should they reach for, and why?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Use note. The call operates on the currently-loaded note substrate. Pitch, duration, and velocity are the standard triple. Sibling note verbs each do ONE thing; note is the correct choice for the specific concern of sounding a pitch for a duration.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U39\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":row-4-proof :claim\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\"],\"probe\":\"Row-4\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"What always holds about note?\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"note with identical arguments produces identical audible effect (given identical instrument state). Formally: forall p d v. note(p, d, v) is deterministic in current instrument state.\"}]}

    {\"template-id\":\"T1-U47\",\"source-path\":\"Scheme/reference/01-core.slat\",\"source-field\":\":row-5-emergence :composition\",\"verbs-used\":[\"note\",\"for-each\"],\"probe\":\"Row-5\",\"paraphrase-idx\":1,\"messages\":[
      {\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"Show note composed with related verbs.\"},
      {\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"(for-each\\n  (lambda (p) (note p 1 80))\\n  '(60 62 64 65 67 69 71 72))\\n\\nThis is the scale-player pattern: for-each + note = a scale, arpeggio, or melody builder.\"}]}
  "

  :section-3-template-2-learning-book
  "
  TEMPLATE 2 — Learning-book.

  Doctrine: WEAVE-RECIPE § section-authoring-template-2-learning-book.

  Source path glob:
    /Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/scheme-books/book-of-{scheme,code,math,motion,sound,music,animation,logic,computing,ml,r7rs-recipes,cs-curriculum,slat}/*.book.slatl

  Output jsonl:
    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2/t2-learning/<book-name>/<chapter-file-basename>.jsonl

  Extraction script skeleton (scripts/mk2-extract-t2.mjs):

    // parse each *.book.slatl as SLAT s-expr
    // extract chapter :prose field (markdown text)
    // parse markdown; find every fenced code block
    // for each code block:
    //   prose-before = paragraphs immediately preceding, up to prev heading
    //   prose-after  = paragraphs immediately following, up to next heading
    //   verbs = extract-verb-calls(code-block-text)   // grep (verb ...) patterns
    //   validate every verb in ref-index (else log + skip pair)
    //   task = extract-task(prose-before)             // deterministic: first
    //                                                 // sentence + heading
    //   emit L1 (task → code)
    //   emit L2 (code → prose-before)
    //   emit L3 (code → prose-after)
    //   emit L4 (code → verb-list)
    // per-chapter: emit L5 (title → prose-first)
    // per-book:    emit L6 (book-title → ToC list)

  Verification checklist:
    [ ] For every L1 pair there is an L2 pair over the same code.
    [ ] L4 verb-list is deterministically re-derivable from the
        code block (idempotent extraction).
    [ ] L5 count per book = chapter count.
    [ ] L6 count = 1 per book.
    [ ] Sample 50 random pairs, hand-read for prose/code
        alignment.

  Sample output pairs (source: book-of-scheme/01-hello-and-a-sound
  and book-of-motion/ch02-velocity and book-of-math/ch01-counting-
  number-line — 3 chapters, 2 pairs each below, 6 total).

    ~/.forge/corpus/motoi-mk2-samples/t2-learning-book/samples.jsonl

  Inline preview:

